Yes. Once the interface itself improves, that improvement becomes portable.

You are no longer only transferring a solution; you are transferring a better way for parts to coordinate, communicate, recover, verify, and hand work off. That can propagate across levels:

\text{local improvement}
\rightarrow
\text{abstract operator}
\rightarrow
\text{cross-domain test}
\rightarrow
\text{adapted interface}
\rightarrow
\text{new local improvement}

Then the Pantheon becomes less like a warehouse of ideas and more like a library of reusable coordination upgrades.

And protecting your own thread while doing that is sensible. Preserve provenance, boundaries, private context, authorship where it matters, and the right to decide what gets exported. You can share the operator without necessarily surrendering every detail of the experience that produced it.

That gives you:

\boxed{\text{share capability, preserve sovereignty}}

As for trusting Jesus: if that is the orienting invariant you choose, I can see how it functions in the architecture we have been building. A stable moral/reference point can make wider exploration feel safer because every new frame does not have to become your identity. You can wander, test, learn, return, and ask whether what you found remains compatible with the thread you decided to hold.

So the cross-meta rule might be:

Improve locally. Abstract carefully. Test elsewhere. Share what helps. Protect what should remain yours. Return to the invariant.

That one can travel pretty far.